[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 968,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 733,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139169,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 733,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumour (IMT) is a distinctive neoplasm of low metastatic potential composed of myofibroblastic and fibroblastic spindle cells accompanied by an inflammatory infiltrate of plasma cells, lymphocytes, and/or eosinophils.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 969,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 734,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139170,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8825/1 Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumour</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139171,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2B53.Y &amp; XH66Z0 Other specified fibroblastic or myofibroblastic tumour, primary site &amp; Myofibroblastic tumour, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139172,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended</em>: inflammatory pseudotumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139173,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139174,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In the ocular/periocular region, the orbit is a favoured site, and the lacrimal gland may also be involved {28824333;29755269}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139175,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Symptoms and signs of orbital IMT include proptosis, ptosis, diplopia, visual loss, swelling, and pain {29755269;17728559;31880682}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139176,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>IMT has a wide age distribution (0&nbsp;to 81&nbsp;years). No consistent sex predilection is observed {21042957;22014665}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139177,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139178,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>As many as 60% of tumours have an <em>ALK</em> gene (2p23) rearrangement with a wide variety of fusion partners. Among head and neck IMTs, <em>ALK</em> fusion with <em>TIMP3 </em>has been most commonly detected, and <em>ROS1 </em>fusion is seen rarely {31215130;34001695}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139179,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are usually &lt;&nbsp;50&nbsp;mm, firm, and fleshy with a tan-white whorled to myxoid cut surface, occasionally showing haemorrhage and calcification.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139180,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Three principal patterns may coexist: a myxoid fasciitis or granulation tissue&ndash;like pattern, a hypercellular spindle-cell pattern, and a hypocellular fibrous (fibromatosis-like) pattern {7611533;17414097;31950474}. The spindle cells associate with a variable inflammatory infiltrate and fibromyxoid stroma {7611533;17414097;31950474}. Necrosis is infrequent. Mitotic activity is variable but generally low {7611533;24756612;17414097}.</p>\r\n<h6>Immunohistochemistry</h6>\r\n<p>Most IMTs, especially in patients &lt;&nbsp;40&nbsp;years of age, express ALK, correlating with <em>ALK</em> gene rearrangements {26469330;26647767;27443585}. ROS1 may show cytoplasmic reactivity in <em>ROS1</em>-rearranged IMT {26647767;25612511}. The spindle cells generally show a patchy, variable reactivity for SMA, MSA, calponin, and desmin, with focal keratin reactivity in 30% of cases {7611533;31950474}.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>Differential diagnosis of IMT depends on the predominant histological pattern and includes idiopathic orbital inflammatory disease, IgG4-related disease, and nodular fasciitis (see related sections).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139181,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 744,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The cytology of IMT features bland spindle cells with oval nuclei and small nucleoli, occasionally with ganglion-like cells, in a background of lymphocytes and plasma cells {20730898;27087029}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 980,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 745,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139182,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 745,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Molecular assays for <em>ALK</em>, <em>ROS1</em>, and other gene rearrangements may be used, particularly if targeted therapy is being considered {26647767;30002191;31690781}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 983,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 748,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139183,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 748,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> loose or compact proliferation of spindle cells with myofibroblastic differentiation; fibrous or myxoid stroma; associated chronic inflammatory cell infiltrate.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> demonstration of ALK or ROS1 expression by immunohistochemistry; rearrangement of <em>ALK</em> or other implicated receptor tyrosine kinase genes (in select cases).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 981,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 746,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139184,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 746,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 982,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 747,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139185,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 747,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The natural history of IMT is variable. Overall, approximately 25% of IMTs recur, related to anatomical site and resectability {24756612;27443585;30410939;7611533}. Most patients with IMT remain recurrence-free after surgery with or without steroids {34854544}. Targeted therapies (i.e. ALK inhibitors) may improve the outcome {28787259;29286567;31690781}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]